+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more integer groups with the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes) and `s` (seconds), such as `2d`, `1h30m`, `90m` or `1d2h3m4s`. Each unit may be used at most once, and
+units must appear in that order. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Spaces, signs and decimals
+are not allowed. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is used as is.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(),
+                  default=datetime.timedelta(minutes=5))
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
